{
  "dom_id": "step-q120265",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
